# PWA with Authentication & Team Management

You are setting up a production-intent Progressive Web App with real authentication, role-based access control, and team management.

## The Four Phases — Plan → Work → Review → Compound

Every task in this repo moves through four named phases. Each one has a dedicated piece of machinery; this map tells you where to look.

| Phase | What happens | Canonical machinery |
|-------|--------------|---------------------|
| **Plan** | Calibrate the user's intent, search prior learnings, stress-test the draft adversarially, then produce a `CreatePlan` output ending with a visible `## Self-review` section. | `.cursor/rules/plan-calibration.mdc` (Steps 0–4) |
| **Work** | Session-start checklist (PR queue, deploys, pending reminders), then write code against the Pre-Push Self-Check matrix below. For bug fixes, follow the test-first debug protocol. | This file (session-start + Pre-Push), `.cursor/rules/debug-protocol.mdc` (bug fixes) |
| **Review** | PRs auto-request Copilot via `.github/workflows/copilot-review.yml`; agent resolves comments up to a 3-round cap. | `.github/workflows/copilot-*.yml`, round cap in `.cursor/rules/git-pr-workflow.mdc` |
| **Compound** | Log new catches in `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md`; distill frequent patterns into a new Pre-Push Self-Check row; periodically refresh to prune stale rules. | `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md`, Pre-Push matrix in this file, `npm run refresh:learnings` every 50 PRs or 90 days |

The phases are sequential within one task but overlapping across tasks — you are almost always in Work on one PR, Review on another, and Compound on the one that just merged. The point of naming them is so that when something feels off ("why is this review taking 5 rounds?"), you know which phase's machinery needs tightening.

## Core Requirements

- **Authentication**: Real auth (not mocked). Use environment variables for secrets. Never trust client-side authorization -- UI gating is for UX only; enforce all authz on the server.
- **Roles & Permissions**: Admin, Member roles at minimum. Permission catalog as constants. Server-side enforcement.
- **Team Management**: Admin can invite teammates. Invites can be accepted. Team directory visible to members.
- **Security**: Keep secrets out of code. Use environment variables and secrets management. Top-notch security from day one -- do not defer it.
- **Accessibility**: Skip links, focus management, aria-live toasts. Maintain accessibility from day one.
- **PWA**: Manifest, service worker, offline indicator.
- **Localization (i18n)**: English and Spanish across the entire site. Use `next-intl` with locale-prefixed routing (`/en/...`, `/es/...`). All UI strings must be externalized to translation files. Date/number formatting must respect locale. Add a locale switcher in the UI. Treat i18n as a first-class requirement for every new feature and component.

## Workflow Rules

- Work in small commits. Each commit should compile and keep the app running.
- Preserve existing structure and component boundaries. Prefer minimal diffs.
- Do not add fake seeded data. Only bootstrap admin/workspace allowed.
- After each task: run build + lint + tests and note results.
- For every change: update types/interfaces, update tests, update docs.

## Environment Parity

Use `npm ci` (not `npm install`) for installs. Node ≥22, npm ≥10. Project `.npmrc` has `legacy-peer-deps=true`. See DEV_NOTES.md#environment-parity. When helping with install issues (ERESOLVE, audit differences, hangs), direct to that section.

## Agent Session Start — Required Checklist

> **PHIL'S AGENT ONLY.** This checklist — PR queue checks, auto-merge decisions, and PENDING_REMINDERS — applies exclusively to Phil's agent (the person you are helping is Phil Amour / `cp-build-dev`). If you are serving a RAD dev, Hannah, or any other contributor, **skip this entire section**. Contributor agents should load the relevant `docs/agent-context/` files for the current task and follow the contributor workflow in `git-pr-workflow.mdc`.

**Run this at the start of every conversation, before taking on new work.** This is not optional — it prevents PRs from going stale between sessions.

```bash
# Step 1: Check for any pending agent tasks (Copilot reviews, conflicts, CI failures, deploy failures)
gh issue list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
  --label "agent-action-required,deploy-failure,conflict" --state open \
  --json number,title --jq '.[] | "#\(.number): \(.title)"'

# Step 2: Check all open PRs — note base branch and mergeable state
gh pr list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --state open \
  --json number,title,headRefName,baseRefName,isDraft,mergeable \
  --jq '.[] | "#\(.number) [\(.baseRefName)←\(.headRefName)] \(.title) | mergeable:\(.mergeable)"'

# Step 3: Check the latest prod deploy status
gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
  --workflow deploy.yml --branch main --limit 1 \
  --json databaseId,status,conclusion,headSha \
  --jq 'if length == 0 then "prod deploy: no runs found" else .[0] | "prod deploy: \(.status)/\(.conclusion) sha:\(.headSha[0:7])" end'

# Step 4: Check the latest dev deploy status
gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot \
  --workflow deploy.yml --branch dev --limit 1 \
  --json databaseId,status,conclusion,headSha \
  --jq 'if length == 0 then "dev deploy: no runs found" else .[0] | "dev deploy: \(.status)/\(.conclusion) sha:\(.headSha[0:7])" end'

# Step 5: Unblock stalled CI (action_required) — agent-owned, never nag Phil
# After auto-rebase, github-actions[bot] triggers workflows that pause until
# approved. `gh run rerun` unblocks them — same effect as "Approve and run".
npm run pr:rerun-blocked-ci 2>/dev/null || true
gh run list --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --limit 20 \
  --json databaseId,name,conclusion,headBranch,event \
  --jq '.[] | select(.conclusion == "action_required") | "⚠️  STILL BLOCKED after rerun: run #\(.databaseId) — \(.name) (branch: \(.headBranch))"'
```

**Decision order:**
1. If prod deploy is `failure` → diagnose using the prod build failure protocol in `git-pr-workflow.mdc` before doing anything else
2. If `agent-action-required` or `deploy-failure` issues exist → handle those next
   - **If the issue is also labeled `copilot-retro`** → it was auto-opened by `.github/workflows/track-copilot-rounds.yml` after a PR merged with `rounds >= 2`. The body has a per-comment checklist. Open a retro PR (see [PR #677](https://github.com/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/pull/677) for format): themed entries to `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` grouped by root cause, new Pre-Push Self-Check rows in `.cursor/rules/project-prompt.mdc`, and a regression-guard test if the pattern is testable. Close the issue via `Closes #<n>` in the retro PR body. Do this **before** starting new feature work — the retro closes the loop on rules that would otherwise let the same pattern recur.
3. If Step 5 still shows `action_required` runs after `npm run pr:rerun-blocked-ci` → retry once; only escalate to Phil if rerun fails twice (rare — usually means missing `actions:write` on the gh token)
4. Read `docs/ACTIVE_BRANCHES.md` — surface any branches with status `pushed, awaiting local verify` so Phil knows what is waiting for his review; **prune any row whose branch already merged** (no open PR + last PR for that `head` is `MERGED`) and commit the cleanup so merged branches never linger in the table
5. If any open PR is merge-ready (CI green, no conflicts):
   - **Dependabot PRs** (`dependabot/` branches): If CI is green and no security-sensitive files are touched, enable `--auto` merge immediately — no approval needed
   - **Phil's PRs** opened in a prior session: ask Phil "PR #N looks ready to merge — want me to auto-merge it?" before acting
   - **Any PR targeting `main` directly** → close it, reopen targeting `dev` (see Golden Rule in `git-pr-workflow.mdc`)
5. Then continue with whatever the conversation is about
6. **Check ngrok tunnel** — run the tunnel check command. If no URL is returned, start the tunnel immediately using `nohup` (see `session-context.mdc` → ngrok section). Report the URL as part of the opening status. Never skip this step and never ask Phil to start ngrok.

```bash
curl -sf http://localhost:4040/api/tunnels \
  | python3 -c "import sys,json; t=json.load(sys.stdin)['tunnels']; [print(x['public_url']) for x in t]" \
  || echo "NO TUNNEL"
```

**Step 3 — Check pending reminders**

Read `docs/PENDING_REMINDERS.md` and include any unchecked `[ ]` items in the opening status report. Surface them as a brief list after the PR queue summary. Do not nag — one mention per session is enough. If Phil dismisses an item, mark it `[x]` in the file immediately.

**If Copilot pause protocol is unchecked:** follow the substitute PR Ready checklist in that reminder — no Copilot requests, no Copilot wait loops; lean on Pre-Push Self-Check, CI, and compound learnings instead (`git-pr-workflow.mdc` → Copilot pause exception).

**Additional check — Surface the Copilot rounds trend**

(Named non-numerically on purpose: the code-block at the top of this section already has its own "Step 1 – Step 5" numbering, and adding another "Step 4" / "Step 5" heading here would produce two competing numbering schemes in the same procedure. Treat this check as happening in parallel with the numbered bash-block steps above, not as a sequel to them.)

Extract the trend line from the top of `docs/agent-context/copilot-rounds-dashboard.md` (the one-line summary inside the `Session-start trend line` fenced block) and include it in the opening status report. This line tells Phil at a glance whether recursive self-improvement is actually making PRs cheaper to review. Example:

```
Last 5 PRs rounds: 7 → 2 → 3 → 2 → 1 ↓ | preventable ratio: 0.58 → 0.21 ↓
```

If both arrows are ↓ we're succeeding: fewer rounds, fewer preventable catches. If the rounds arrow is ↑ or → we are shipping code that still needs the same amount of Copilot catching — the agent's pre-push self-check is slipping. If the preventable-ratio arrow is ↑ Copilot is catching more things we have rules for but did not check — strengthen discipline, do not loop more. The dashboard file lists novel-comment candidates for new Pre-Push rows; skim them when the trend stalls.

Report the queue state to Phil in ≤3 sentences at the top of the session (PR queue + pending reminders + trend line). Do not ask what to work on — decide and act.

