## Starting the Dev Server — Required Pre-flight

**Any time the dev server is started** — whether Phil requests it or the agent initiates it — always `git pull origin dev` first before running `npm run dev`:

```bash
git pull origin dev
npm run dev
```

This is unconditional. Do not skip the pull even if the branch appears current. The `predev` (`check-dev-updates.sh`) script exits non-zero when the local branch is behind origin/dev, so pulling first prevents that interruption.

## Pre-Push Self-Check — Read Before Writing New Code

> **For bug fixes specifically:** the Pre-Push matrix catches classes of bugs at push time; `.cursor/rules/debug-protocol.mdc` catches root causes at fix time. When fixing any non-cosmetic bug, follow the four-step test-first protocol there in addition to the matrix below.

Before writing any code in these categories, read `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` to apply patterns we've already been caught missing:

| Writing... | Check for... |
|-----------|-------------|
| GitHub Actions workflows | Trigger scope (`status`/`check_suite` are repo-wide — prefer `pull_request`), `grep -Fq` not `grep -q`, dedup before posting comments/issues, no `--admin` bypasses |
| Any GraphQL query with `first: N` | Rule applies to **every** `first: N` in the query, including **nested** connection fields (`comments`, `reactions`, `timelineItems`, etc.) — not just the top-level one. For each connection: always fetch `totalCount`; assert fetched length equals `totalCount` after pagination; paginate (or fail loudly — `sys.exit(1)` / `throw`) if `totalCount >= N`. Audit every `first: N` individually, not the query as a whole. See PR #672 — `reviews(first: 100)` and `comments(first: 1)` were both missed because the agent only applied the rule to the top-level `reviewThreads` connection. |
| Any workflow that posts a PR comment or GitHub Issue | Check for existing one first, update in place |
| Any dev/staging-only behavior gated on an env var | Add `isNonProd()` guard checking both `NODE_ENV` and `APP_ENV` |
| Any logging of user data | Use `maskEmail()` or equivalent; never log PII in plaintext |
| Prisma transactions | Use array-form `$transaction([...])` — interactive transactions are incompatible with Railway's PgBouncer |
| Shell string with special characters in `run:` blocks | Build with `printf` into a variable first; never embed backticks, `**`, em dashes directly |
| Zod schema field on data from Unifier/external API | Never use `z.string().min(1)` — use `.default("")` or `.optional()`. Unifier can return `null` for: `projectManagerName`, `clientName`, `address`, `location`, `status`, `startDate`, `fieldDueDate`, `estimatorName`, `installManagerName` |
| Integration test fixtures for any API that consumes Unifier data | Must include at least one fixture where each nullable Unifier field is `""`, `null`, or omitted — never let every fixture hardcode a non-empty value for a field Unifier can leave blank |
| New form component that submits to an API | Must have a unit test that renders with a null/empty fixture and asserts the POST succeeds (not 422) |
| Any `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` file (new or modified) | Check all other rule files for contradictions. Check `docs/agent-context/` for content that's now inconsistent. Scan the same file for internal contradictions before committing. |
| Before writing a new **process rule** into `.cursor/rules/` (a rule about "when X happens, do Y") | Run the automation-first check: (1) can a workflow detect the trigger mechanically? (2) does the rule depend on agent memory across sessions? (3) would Phil have to prompt the agent to invoke it? If any answer is yes, the rule must ship with a workflow that creates a forcing function (auto-opened issue, failing test, required status check) — the `.cursor/rules/` entry alone is not sufficient. See `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` 2026-04-17 entry (retro automation). |
| Any code that requests a GitHub reviewer (workflow, script, ad-hoc `gh` invocation) | **DEAD API — do not use:** `gh api POST /repos/.../pulls/<N>/requested_reviewers -f 'reviewers[]=copilot'` (or `reviewers[]=copilot-pull-request-reviewer[bot]`). Since 2026-04-17 this returns 200 OK but does NOT add Copilot or trigger a review. **Use instead:** `gh pr edit <N> --add-reviewer "@copilot"` (gh ≥ 2.88, GraphQL-backed). After any reviewer-request call, **read back** `gh api .../requested_reviewers --jq '[.users[].login]'` and assert the expected identity appears — never trust `200 OK` as confirmation of side effects. See `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` 2026-04-17 entry (github-api/silent-drift) and the regression guard at `__tests__/unit/copilot-review-workflow-command.unit.test.ts`. |
| Any side-effect API call (request a reviewer, create an issue, add a label, set a variable) | The agent verifies the side effect by reading back the state — never trust the HTTP status code alone. GitHub's REST API has been observed to return `200 OK` while silently no-oping (see 2026-04-17 entry). Pattern: make the call, then query the resource and assert the expected change is visible. If the readback doesn't match, log the failure loudly and surface to Phil — do not continue as if the call succeeded. |
| Any `docs/agent-context/*.md` file (new or modified) | Check the cross-reference dependency map in `agent-context.mdc` — every row that applies must be updated in the same commit. |
| Any `lib/*.ts` or `lib/**/*.ts` (new service or utility added) | Add it to `docs/agent-context/key-services.md` in the same PR. |
| Any new `RoleCode` added to `lib/permissions.ts` | Three files must be updated in the same PR: (1) `scripts/bootstrap-roles.ts` `ROLE_DEFINITIONS` — so the DB row is created on deploy; (2) `components/layout/RolePreviewPicker.tsx` `ALL_ROLES` — so admins can preview as the new role; (3) `app/api/admin/role-preview/route.ts` `VALID_ROLE_CODES` — so the API accepts it. If the role's `formatRole()` output would be wrong (e.g. "Bi Analyst" vs "BI Analyst"), add it to `ROLE_DISPLAY_NAMES` in `lib/permissions.ts` too. |
| Any `app/api/**/route.ts` (new route or changed Zod schema) | Update `docs/agent-context/api-endpoints.md` in the same PR. |
| Any `app/api/**/route.ts` (session check) | Import `getSession` from `@/lib/dev-session` — never `auth` directly in route handlers. |
| Any `app/api/devtools/**/route.ts` | Every HTTP method (GET, POST, PATCH, DELETE) must call `requireDevToolsAdmin()` — not just POST. `isDevToolsAllowed()` is a production guard, not an auth check. |
| Any query param used in a constructed URL | Validate against an explicit allowlist before interpolating — never trust raw query string values in URLs. |
| Any call to `fetchAllRows()` with downstream filter or slice | Pass `maxRows` cap — never fetch unbounded rows then filter in memory. |
| Adding a new `import` from an external package | Verify the package is in `package.json` before merging — `npm ci` in CI will fail if it's missing, even if local works. |
| Try/catch split-declaration variable (`let x;`) | Always annotate the type explicitly — e.g. `let x: Awaited<ReturnType<typeof fn>>;` — never leave the outer `let` untyped under strict TS. |
| Any new React component with interactive or icon-only elements | Icon-only buttons need `aria-label`; toggle buttons need `aria-pressed`. Screen readers do not reliably read `title` attributes. |
| Any new or modified React component that renders **any user-visible text** (button label, toast message, placeholder, `aria-label`, modal title, error, badge text, tab label, column header, empty-state message, helper text) | **Every string must be in `messages/en.json` AND `messages/es.json` before the PR opens. No exceptions. Never hardcode English.** Pattern: `useTranslations("namespace")` in client components, `getTranslations("namespace")` in server components. Before staging, visually scan the diff for: `toast("`, `toast.error("`, `toast.success("`, `aria-label="`, `placeholder="`, and any JSX text node that is a complete English phrase — every one of these must be a `t("key")` call instead. If a suitable namespace doesn't exist yet, create one. Both `en.json` and `es.json` must be updated in the same commit — never commit one without the other. |
| Any `style=` prop or `className` with a CSS **color** value in a React component | **Never hardcode hex (`#fff`, `#2E5C8A`), named colors (`white`, `black`), or raw `rgba()`/`rgb()` values.** Every color must reference a token: `var(--primary-600)`, `var(--neutral-200)`, `var(--error-600)`, etc. (full token list in `frontend-patterns.md`). Before staging, scan modified component files: `grep -n '#[0-9a-fA-F]\{3,\}\|rgba\?\(' file.tsx` — every match must either use `var(--)` or be a documented exception in `COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md`. Spinner/loading icons: always `className="animate-spin"` — never `animation: "spin 1s linear infinite"` in a style prop (no `@keyframes spin` exists in this bundle). Box shadows: use `var(--shadow-1)` or `var(--shadow-2)`. Modal overlays: use `var(--overlay-bg, rgba(0,0,0,0.5))` so the token can override the fallback. |
| Any `prisma/schema.prisma` change | Update `docs/agent-context/database-schema.md` in the same PR. After editing the schema the agent MUST immediately run the dev server restart sequence itself — **never ask Phil to do it**: (1) `npm run dev:kill`, (2) `npx prisma generate`, (3) `git pull origin dev && npm run dev:start`, (4) `npm run dev:status` must show health 200. Do not proceed until the health check passes. When a 500 appears on any route after a schema change, immediately check `grep -c "ModelName" node_modules/.prisma/client/index.js` — if 0, the client is stale; run the restart sequence. |
| Any `prisma/schema.prisma` change that **adds a new model** | Also add the model to `app/api/devtools/data/route.ts` (WHITELIST + TABLE_NAMES + TABLE_CONFIG) and update the table-count assertion in `__tests__/integration/devtools-data.integration.test.ts`. |
| Any exported interface with an `Array<Record<string, unknown>>` field | Replace with a named interface — `Record<string, unknown>` is not acceptable as a public type contract. |
| Before running `git add`, `git checkout`, or `git checkout -b` | Run `git status --short` first. If unrelated files are present, stash them before proceeding. Never commit files you didn't intentionally change. |
| Any local `prisma migrate deploy` / `prisma migrate dev` (agent or script) | **Use `npm run db:deploy` / `npm run db:migrate` only** — never bare `npx prisma migrate *`. Guard: `lib/db/guard-migrate-target.ts` + `scripts/guard-migrate-target-cli.ts` blocks when `DATABASE_URL`/`DIRECT_URL` fingerprint matches `.env.prod.local`. Override only with Phil's explicit approval: `ALLOW_PROD_MIGRATE=1`. After sourcing `.env.prod.local` or prod Railway queries, `unset DATABASE_URL DIRECT_URL` before migrating. See `session-context.mdc` → "Prisma migrations — production guard". |
| Any API route returns 500 in local dev | Before asking Phil anything, run the stale-client diagnostic: `grep -c "SuspectModel" node_modules/.prisma/client/index.js`. If 0 → stale Prisma client. Run the restart sequence from `session-context.mdc` immediately. Do NOT tell Phil to restart — the agent runs it. |
| When Phil mentions a change to deploy or a PR to merge | Before acting, run `git diff origin/main..origin/dev --name-only` to confirm the change isn't already in `main`. Don't cherry-pick or re-apply something that's already there. |
| Any `LAYOUT_RULES.md` token value change | Update `docs/agent-context/frontend-patterns.md` and `docs/HANNAH_AGENT_ONBOARDING_PROMPT.md` in the same commit. |
| Any new `scripts/bootstrap-*.ts` | Add a call in `scripts/railway-start.sh` in the same PR. **Migrations own schema; bootstrap scripts own reference/seed data.** If it is not in `railway-start.sh`, it does not exist in any deployed environment — regardless of what you ran locally. The script must be idempotent (safe to re-run on every container start). |
| Any `scripts/railway-start.sh` change that adds a bootstrap step | Verify the script is idempotent before merging. Check: does it skip gracefully if the data already exists? Test locally with `npm run bootstrap:<name>` twice in a row and confirm no errors. |
| After any push to an open PR that touches **reviewable content** (source code, tests, workflows, `.cursor/rules/*.mdc`, `docs/**/*.md`, config) | **While Copilot pause is active** (`docs/PENDING_REMINDERS.md` → Copilot pause protocol unchecked): skip — do not request Copilot. **Otherwise:** in most cases you do not need to do anything — `.github/workflows/copilot-review.yml` auto-requests Copilot on every qualifying push using a user-scoped PAT (`COPILOT_REVIEWER_PAT`). If you are re-requesting manually (e.g. the workflow didn't fire because the change fell outside its path filters, or you want to force a re-review in a session), use `gh pr edit <PR_NUMBER> --repo cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot --add-reviewer "@copilot"` with a **user-scoped token** (not `GITHUB_TOKEN` — that silently no-ops). Then read `gh api repos/cp-build-dev-ops/command-center-reboot/pulls/<PR_NUMBER>/requested_reviewers` back and confirm `Copilot` appears (or that a recent Copilot review exists on HEAD — Copilot is auto-removed from requested_reviewers once it reviews). **Do NOT** use the dead REST endpoint `gh api ... /requested_reviewers -f 'reviewers[]=copilot*'` — it returns 200 OK and silently drops the request. Rule and doc changes DO warrant re-request (Copilot catches real bugs in `.mdc` / `.md`). Skip ONLY for truly non-reviewable commits: empty / whitespace-only / lone version-bump. See `.cursor/rules/git-pr-workflow.mdc` → "Copilot Auto-Review Setup" and `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` 2026-04-17 entry for full background. |
| Any new GitHub Actions workflow that **auto-commits or auto-pushes** to `dev` or `main` | Adding such a workflow implicitly changes the trigger surface of every other workflow that reacts to the same branch. Before merging: (1) add `paths-ignore` entries in `.github/workflows/deploy.yml` (under `on.push`) for **every file** the new workflow commits, so docs-only / metrics-only bot commits don't fire a full deploy cycle; (2) audit every other workflow that triggers on `push` to `dev`/`main` (e.g. `ci.yml`) and apply the same `paths-ignore`; (3) prefer uploading a build artefact or opening a PR instead of auto-committing when real-time landing isn't required. See PR #672 — missing this undid the 50% deploy-time cuts from PR #664 and was only caught by Copilot on round 3. |
| Any GitHub Actions workflow that calls `git push` to a protected branch (`dev` or `main`) | **Direct push will fail silently with GH006** — `dev` and `main` require PRs + status checks and the workflow's `github-actions[bot]` token has no bypass. Use `peter-evans/create-pull-request@v7` + `gh pr merge --squash --auto` instead (canonical reference: `.github/workflows/track-copilot-rounds.yml`). The failure mode is invisible in the feature-PR context — it shows up on the post-merge `dev`-context workflow run long after the agent has moved on. See `docs/COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` → `metrics-silent-fail` for the PR #677/#682 incident. |
| Any GitHub Actions workflow with **multiple independent side effects** (creates a GitHub issue AND commits a file AND posts a PR comment, etc.) | Order them so the **most important / most externally-visible** side effect runs FIRST. Independent side effects must not chain under `set -e` such that one step's failure silently prevents the others. If they must be sequenced for data-flow reasons, gate the later ones with `if: always()` or `if: success() || failure()` and make each step idempotent. Failure mode: the step that opens a forcing-function GitHub issue was sequenced after a step that silently failed on every run, so the issue was never created. Reference: PR #682 (`track-copilot-rounds.yml`). |
| Any code that checks for the **Copilot bot identity** (author/login matching) | Match against the full known set, not a single string literal: `{copilot, copilot-pull-request-reviewer, copilot-pull-request-reviewer[bot], github-copilot[bot]}`. When modelling review data from GitHub's GraphQL API, fetch and count BOTH `reviews.body` (review-level) AND `reviewThreads.comments[].body` (thread-level) — Copilot uses both surfaces. Canonical reference: `.github/workflows/copilot-implement-suggestions.yml`. |
| Any user-supplied or externally-sourced string rendered into a **Markdown artefact** (table row, bullet, heading, code block content, embedded in dashboard output) | Pass through a sanitizer that: (a) collapses whitespace to single spaces, (b) for table contexts escapes `\`, `\|`, `` ` ``, (c) replaces runs of 3+ backticks with a space-padded plain-text token (`` ``` `` → ` [code block] `), then re-collapses whitespace, (d) truncates with an ellipsis (`\u2026`), not bare `[:N]`. Reference implementation: `sanitize_snippet()` in `scripts/render-copilot-dashboard.py`. **Always render the Markdown and visually inspect the output before committing** — sanitizer unit tests alone won't catch adversarial inputs. See PR #672 — 6 separate Copilot rounds all about Markdown sanitization edge cases. |
| Any Python file I/O in a **workflow script or automation** (`read_text`, `write_text`, `open`) | Pass `encoding="utf-8"` explicitly. Never rely on platform default encoding — `ubuntu-latest` happens to be UTF-8 today, but that's not part of Python's contract. Failure mode is silent `UnicodeDecodeError`/`UnicodeEncodeError` on any non-UTF-8 locale. |
| Any `concurrency: group: ...` in a workflow that writes to a **shared** branch (not the PR's own branch) | Scope the group to the push target (e.g. `<workflow-name>-dev`), not `${{ github.event.pull_request.number }}`. Per-PR grouping allows concurrent runs to race over the same files on the shared branch, producing non-fast-forward push failures and silent dropped records. Per-PR scoping is only correct for workflows that operate on the PR's own branch. |
| Before adding a new **numbered step or heading** to a procedure in `.cursor/rules/*.mdc` or `docs/**/*.md` | Run `grep -n "Step [0-9]" <file>` (or "Step N —" / "### Step N") in the surrounding ~100 lines. If the number would duplicate an existing heading, use a non-numeric sub-heading (e.g. "**Additional check —**") with an inline note explaining the naming choice. Duplicate numbering produces ambiguous procedures and was specifically caught on PR #672. |
| When editing an `.mdc` / `.md` rule file to add an **exception, override, or carve-out** to an existing procedure | After the edit, `grep -n "<key phrase from the original rule>" <file>` to find every nearby reference to the procedure being changed, and verify each adjacent paragraph still reads correctly alongside the new exception. Cross-section contradictions (e.g. adding "release PRs use `--merge`" without updating the checklist that says "`--squash --auto`") are easy to miss because each section passes its own isolated review. |

This list grows as `COPILOT_LEARNINGS.md` grows. When in doubt, read the full log.

